<Base title="Choosing a relational database" description="Compare SQLite, PostgreSQL, MySQL and SQL Server through deployment, transaction behaviour, access patterns and the work of operating your application.">
 <article class="visual-essay relational-design">
  <header class="essay-header"><p class="eyebrow">Databases and storage</p><h1>Choosing a relational database</h1><div class="essay-meta"><span>Relational deep dive</span><a href="/learn/databases/relational/">Relational introduction ↗</a></div></header>
  <p>A relational database gives an application tables, queries and a way to keep related changes together. Choosing a product also chooses where those operations run, how concurrent requests interact, and what someone must do when the machine fails. Two products can both support transactions while giving the same application code different waiting and retry behaviour.</p>
  <p>Start with the application you need to operate. A field notebook that works without a network, a shared order service and an established reporting estate can all benefit from relational data. They need different arrangements around it. A useful comparison follows those differences through the work, rather than counting which product has more features.</p>
  <p>This comparison uses PostgreSQL 18, MySQL 8.4 with InnoDB, SQL Server 2025 and SQLite’s documented embedded model, checked on 15 October 2026. These are identified comparison targets, not a claim that every deployment uses the same defaults or that newer releases cannot alter a decision.</p>
  <nav aria-label="In this article"><ol><li><a href="#deployment">Where the database runs</a></li><li><a href="#correctness">What the application can assume</a></li><li><a href="#access">How records are accessed</a></li><li><a href="#operations">What you will operate</a></li><li><a href="#decision">Making the choice concrete</a></li></ol></nav>
  <section id="deployment"><h2>Where the database runs</h2>
   <p>A field notebook stores observations on a laptop and must open them while offline. With SQLite, the application calls a library in its own process; that library reads and writes local database files. There is no separate database server to discover, authenticate to or keep running. The application still needs a backup and synchronisation design if the observations must survive losing the laptop or be shared with colleagues.</p>
   <p>A shared order service has another shape. Several application instances send requests to a database server that coordinates their access. PostgreSQL, MySQL and SQL Server serve this role. The network adds a possible failure and a round trip, but it gives the applications one managed place to access the shared data. Authentication, resource limits and operational tools belong around that service.</p>
   <p>SQLite can also sit behind an application server. Website visitors do not each need a direct database connection: the application can accept HTTP requests and perform local SQL. The decisive question is which processes need to write the same database and where they run. SQLite’s <a href="https://www.sqlite.org/whentouse.html">deployment guidance</a> explains why directly sharing its database file between many computers over a network filesystem is a different and troublesome arrangement.</p>
   <p>In SQLite, writes to one database are serialised. WAL mode lets readers continue alongside a writer, but does not create multiple simultaneous writers to that file. A queue of short edits may work well; a long write transaction makes other writers wait. Its <a href="https://www.sqlite.org/isolation.html">isolation documentation</a> also describes the case where a reader must restart before becoming a writer. Measure the actual write transactions, rather than treating either “one writer” or “many users” as a complete capacity verdict.</p>
   <div class="table-scroll" role="region" tabindex="0" aria-label="Deployment starting points"><table><thead><tr><th>Application need</th><th>Useful starting point</th><th>What could change it</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>One device, offline edits</td><td>SQLite embedded in the application</td><td>Shared ownership, synchronisation and concurrent writing across machines</td></tr><tr><td>Shared service with multiple application instances</td><td>A server database with an understood operating model</td><td>Actual SQL behaviour, team skills, availability requirements and service constraints</td></tr><tr><td>Existing SQL Server applications and reporting</td><td>Evaluate the established SQL Server deployment first</td><td>A specific unsupported requirement or measured cost that justifies migration</td></tr><tr><td>Existing PostgreSQL or MySQL service</td><td>Test the new workload on its current engine first</td><td>Correctness, capacity or operational limits that a candidate demonstrably resolves</td></tr></tbody></table></div>
   <p>These are ways to narrow an investigation. An existing system is valuable because its behaviour, tools and recovery procedures are already known. That advantage needs to be weighed against a real limitation, rather than assuming either a clean start or a migration is free.</p>
  </section>
  <section id="correctness"><h2>What the application can assume</h2>
   <p>Consider an order screen that issues two ordinary reads in one transaction. Another connection commits a new order between them. Should the second read see it? That question has a more useful answer than “the database supports ACID.” The answer depends on the isolation level and what kinds of reads the application uses.</p>
   <p>At PostgreSQL’s default Read Committed level, each ordinary query obtains its own snapshot. MySQL 8.4 InnoDB defaults to Repeatable Read; its ordinary consistent reads in a transaction use the snapshot established by the first such read. Locking reads and writes need their own analysis in both engines. The respective <a href="https://www.postgresql.org/docs/18/transaction-iso.html">PostgreSQL</a> and <a href="https://dev.mysql.com/doc/refman/8.4/en/innodb-transaction-isolation-levels.html">InnoDB</a> documentation supplies the exact cases. Moving the same two SELECT statements between products without choosing their isolation can change the screen’s view of time.</p>
   <p>SQL Server’s Read Committed behaviour also depends on the database’s <code>READ_COMMITTED_SNAPSHOT</code> setting. With it off, shared locks protect ordinary reads; with it on, statement-level row versions supply their committed view. This setting changes reader/writer interactions even though the application still requests “Read Committed.” Microsoft’s <a href="https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/sql/t-sql/statements/set-transaction-isolation-level-transact-sql?view=sql-server-ver17">isolation reference</a> distinguishes that statement view from a Snapshot transaction. Check the chosen hosted service’s settings as well as the engine name.</p>
   <p>Make the contract observable. In two test connections, arrange the read, committed write and second read in a known order. Then try two attempts to reserve the last item. Record the value each caller sees, where it waits, the error it receives and which part of the operation it retries. The collection’s <a href="/learn/databases/relational/isolation/">isolation</a> and <a href="/learn/databases/relational/conflicts-and-retries/">retry</a> articles provide the reasoning; the scripts must use each candidate’s syntax and actual connection settings.</p>
   <p>Data conversion deserves equally concrete cases. A column’s type name does not tell you everything about which input it accepts. Ordinary SQLite tables use type affinity; <a href="https://www.sqlite.org/stricttables.html">STRICT tables</a> provide a more restrictive, explicitly selected model with their own permitted type names. In MySQL, <a href="https://dev.mysql.com/doc/refman/8.4/en/sql-mode.html">SQL mode</a> affects invalid-value handling. For SQLite, explicitly enable and check <a href="https://www.sqlite.org/foreignkeys.html">foreign-key enforcement</a> for the connections that require it. A schema printed in a migration file does not establish what every runtime connection enforces.</p>
   <p>The <a href="/learn/databases/relational/sandbox/?tutorial=product-contract">product-contract lesson</a> gives one small PostgreSQL case. Its line table has a positive, required integer quantity and <code>numeric(6,2)</code> unit price. PostgreSQL rounds an input price to that scale before storing it:</p>
   <Sql code={amount} label="Observe the stored value, not only whether the insert succeeded" />
   <p>The stored price is 18.01 and the total is 36.02. If the application requires rejecting a third decimal place, accepting this insert is the wrong outcome even though the stored value fits its column. The lesson also sends a zero quantity and checks the surviving row after rejection. It runs PostgreSQL only; use the same intended outcomes to write candidate-specific tests, rather than treating it as evidence about MySQL, SQL Server or SQLite.</p>
   <p>Include duplicate keys, absent values, invalid references, time zones and the application’s important string comparisons. A case-insensitive identifier rule, for example, must handle <code>Ada</code> and <code>ada</code> deliberately. Test with the actual collation and application driver. The relevant result is whether the application preserves its meaning, not whether a schema can be translated without a syntax error.</p>
  </section>
  <section id="access"><h2>How records are accessed</h2>
   <p>The same logical primary key can imply different physical work. PostgreSQL’s ordinary heap table stores its records separately from the B-tree that usually enforces the primary key. An index lookup can therefore lead to a table-page visit. The <a href="/learn/databases/relational/storage/">storage article</a> follows that arrangement; it should not be assumed to describe every relational engine.</p>
   <p>InnoDB normally organises the table through its primary-key clustered index, whose leaf records contain the row data. Its secondary indexes include the primary-key value as the row locator. A lookup through a secondary key can therefore require a second search through the clustered index. A long primary key also adds to those secondary entries. The <a href="https://dev.mysql.com/doc/refman/8.4/en/innodb-index-types.html">InnoDB index documentation</a> explains the fallback choices when no suitable primary key is declared. This is a concrete reason to evaluate key width and access patterns together.</p>
   <p>SQL Server offers heap tables and clustered rowstore indexes, as well as columnstore arrangements for suitable workloads. In a clustered rowstore, the clustered key supplies the locator used by nonclustered indexes; a primary key and a clustered index are related design choices, not synonyms. Microsoft’s <a href="https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/sql/relational-databases/sql-server-index-design-guide?view=sql-server-ver17">index design guide</a> develops those distinctions. A reporting workload may warrant a different layout inside the current product before it warrants another service.</p>
   <p>These mechanisms suggest tests. For an order service, load representative headers and lines, preserve the real key widths and distributions, and run both customer lookups and writes. Inspect pages visited, result size and write cost. A plan in one engine is an explanation of that engine’s work; the different plan names in another are not a ranking. The <a href="/learn/databases/relational/slow-queries/">performance investigation</a> explains how to compare the whole request under equivalent traffic.</p>
   <p>Broader query facilities can change application work too. PostgreSQL has built-in <a href="https://www.postgresql.org/docs/18/datatype-json.html">JSON types and indexing</a>; MySQL 8.4 has its own <a href="https://dev.mysql.com/doc/refman/8.4/en/json.html">JSON representation and access facilities</a>; SQL Server has <a href="https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/sql/relational-databases/json/json-data-sql-server?view=sql-server-ver17">version-dependent JSON facilities</a>. “Supports JSON” does not make their operators, validation, indexing or migration behaviour interchangeable. Test the operations the application needs, including writes to frequently changing attributes.</p>
  </section>
  <section id="operations"><h2>What you will operate</h2>
   <p>An engine, its commercial edition and a hosted service are different choices. A hosted PostgreSQL service can restrict extensions and administrative privileges. Two MySQL offerings can provide different backup and failover workflows around the same engine. SQL Server editions have different resource and feature boundaries. Check the <a href="https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/sql/sql-server/editions-and-components-of-sql-server-2025?view=sql-server-ver17">SQL Server 2025 edition matrix</a> against the exact availability and maintenance operations you will use; Developer editions are for development and testing, not a shortcut to a production licence.</p>
   <p>Compare a recovery procedure, not just a backup checkbox. Create representative data, take the proposed backup, restore it to a separate destination, and validate the application there. Measure how much recent work is missing and when the service is usable. For a hosted product, include access to encryption keys, network permissions and the time required to provision the destination. The <a href="/learn/databases/relational/backup-and-restore/">recovery guide</a> shows how those questions become a drill.</p>
   <p>Do the same for failover and schema evolution. A service that promotes a replica still requires the application to reconnect and resolve an uncertain request. An edition that supports an online operation may still impose locks at particular stages. Rehearse the actual operation with long-lived transactions and old/new application versions present. Record the privileges it needs; an operation available in a local administrator’s session may be unavailable to the migration identity in production.</p>
   <p>Operating cost includes the people and practices already available. A team that restores SQL Server routinely has an advantage there; a PostgreSQL service with tested automation has a corresponding advantage. Include hosting, storage, replica and backup retention, support, required edition and migration work in the comparison. Verify licence terms for the intended use and distribution. There is no useful universal cost ranking without those conditions.</p>
  </section>
  <section id="decision"><h2>Making the choice concrete</h2>
   <p>For the offline notebook, begin with SQLite and test its local write duration, backup process and eventual synchronisation needs. Moving to a server solely to obtain SQL would add a network requirement the application was meant to avoid. If several sites must reserve shared stock, however, a local file on each device does not by itself coordinate that stock; the ownership problem needs a different design.</p>
   <p>For a new shared order service, compare PostgreSQL, MySQL/InnoDB and SQL Server against the same purchase and recovery contract. A useful prototype includes the real driver, connection pool, schema, repeated request handling and representative skew. Use identical business outcomes and traffic, while allowing an appropriate schema and indexes for each engine. Copying one engine’s physical design unchanged into every candidate is not a neutral comparison.</p>
   <p>For an established application, ask what the proposed move fixes. If the problem is an ORM issuing hundreds of relationship queries, changing engines may carry that pattern with it. If a required facility or operational constraint cannot be met credibly in the current system, a migration may be justified—but its data conversion, coexistence, rollback and operating training belong in the cost.</p>
   <p>Write down the decision with its conditions: which engine/version and service, which correctness cases passed, what load was measured, how recovery was tested, and which assumption would make you reconsider. That gives a future engineer something more useful than a preferred brand. It explains why this arrangement fits the application and where its limits have actually been examined.</p>
  </section>
